WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.968
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:06.950 --> 00:00:09.710
DINO CHIESA: Many
companies are on a journey

00:00:09.710 --> 00:00:13.250
evolving their monoliths
to microservices

00:00:13.250 --> 00:00:16.219
or even managed microservices.

00:00:16.219 --> 00:00:19.880
And I'll say many companies,
but you can't really

00:00:19.880 --> 00:00:22.010
think of a single company
as on this journey.

00:00:22.010 --> 00:00:23.677
You have a lot of
different departments,

00:00:23.677 --> 00:00:26.030
a lot of different
projects, and each of them

00:00:26.030 --> 00:00:28.760
are in a different
place on that journey.

00:00:28.760 --> 00:00:31.520
Some projects, some
companies started

00:00:31.520 --> 00:00:32.780
from someplace different.

00:00:32.780 --> 00:00:35.300
Maybe they started from a
service oriented architecture,

00:00:35.300 --> 00:00:37.580
which is sort of a
half step along the way

00:00:37.580 --> 00:00:38.940
towards microservices.

00:00:38.940 --> 00:00:42.170
In microservices architectural
pattern, as you all know,

00:00:42.170 --> 00:00:46.100
an application is composed
of finer grained services

00:00:46.100 --> 00:00:51.500
that intercommunicate via these
lightweight, simple mechanisms

00:00:51.500 --> 00:00:53.540
that are called APIs.

00:00:53.540 --> 00:00:54.950
The goals of this--

00:00:54.950 --> 00:00:58.430
increase product velocity,
manage complexity of code,

00:00:58.430 --> 00:01:01.220
perhaps polyglot
service construction,

00:01:01.220 --> 00:01:03.870
where you've got
multiple languages,

00:01:03.870 --> 00:01:08.510
different languages being used
to implement different services

00:01:08.510 --> 00:01:11.510
and working together,
and empowering developers

00:01:11.510 --> 00:01:14.280
in order to advance
business goals more rapidly.

00:01:14.280 --> 00:01:16.410
So that's all familiar to you.

00:01:16.410 --> 00:01:18.110
There is an
alternative, of course,

00:01:18.110 --> 00:01:21.590
to migrating a monolith
in decomposing it

00:01:21.590 --> 00:01:23.840
into a set of microservices,
and that is just wrap it.

00:01:23.840 --> 00:01:27.170
Just wrap it in a
service or in an API,

00:01:27.170 --> 00:01:28.610
and call into that directly.

00:01:28.610 --> 00:01:31.340
Just kind of freeze dry it
that way and get access to it.

00:01:31.340 --> 00:01:34.490
Either way, it's a service, it's
just a coarser grain service,

00:01:34.490 --> 00:01:36.740
and you don't need to
touch things as much.

00:01:36.740 --> 00:01:40.160
In fact, in the
keynote yesterday, we

00:01:40.160 --> 00:01:43.220
saw Jennifer Lind describing
this exact scenario

00:01:43.220 --> 00:01:46.220
with one of the tools that works
with Anthos, where you can take

00:01:46.220 --> 00:01:50.660
a VM, where you've got a system
already running, run this tool,

00:01:50.660 --> 00:01:53.600
and it pulls it into a
container and makes it hostable

00:01:53.600 --> 00:01:57.630
in Kubernetes Engine, regardless
of where you want to run it.

00:01:57.630 --> 00:02:02.690
So you wrap it maybe one step
along the path of the Strangler

00:02:02.690 --> 00:02:05.000
pattern, if you're
familiar with that.

00:02:05.000 --> 00:02:07.040
Wrap that monolith
and just put it

00:02:07.040 --> 00:02:09.050
wherever you want
in that container.

00:02:09.050 --> 00:02:11.390
So a couple of different
options, neither are wrong.

00:02:11.390 --> 00:02:13.880
Many companies have
different projects

00:02:13.880 --> 00:02:19.360
that are traversing this
path in different directions.

00:02:19.360 --> 00:02:21.690
The key thing to remember is
APIs are the communication

00:02:21.690 --> 00:02:22.190
contracts.

00:02:22.190 --> 00:02:25.140
So between the services
that are inter-cooperating,

00:02:25.140 --> 00:02:28.140
APIs are the things that allow
those things to communicate

00:02:28.140 --> 00:02:31.770
between clumps of services
or clusters of services--

00:02:31.770 --> 00:02:34.500
maybe you've got them structured
in different meshes more

00:02:34.500 --> 00:02:36.030
or less formally managed--

00:02:36.030 --> 00:02:37.090
APIs are used.

00:02:37.090 --> 00:02:40.440
And again, between clients that
are external to your network,

00:02:40.440 --> 00:02:42.160
to your meshes, it's API.

00:02:42.160 --> 00:02:44.910
So APIs are the interface,
not the implementation.

00:02:44.910 --> 00:02:48.450
APIs are the things that
allow people, parties

00:02:48.450 --> 00:02:50.790
to interchange information.

00:02:50.790 --> 00:02:54.090
Some of these APIs may be--
a really common structure

00:02:54.090 --> 00:02:57.420
for APIs is HTTP1 and
Rest, and maybe you're

00:02:57.420 --> 00:02:59.670
going to use JSON
for your data format.

00:02:59.670 --> 00:03:02.548
That is not presupposed
by the rest of what

00:03:02.548 --> 00:03:03.840
we're going to talk about here.

00:03:03.840 --> 00:03:05.882
That's a common one, but
that's not the only one.

00:03:05.882 --> 00:03:10.200
And API is just a programmatic
way to access the system.

00:03:10.200 --> 00:03:12.390
So we see a lot of
people using HTTP2 now,

00:03:12.390 --> 00:03:17.370
we see people using GRPC, we see
people using GraphQL, and lots

00:03:17.370 --> 00:03:19.290
of other options as well.

00:03:19.290 --> 00:03:22.690
So API is just the
communication mechanism.

00:03:22.690 --> 00:03:25.950
So I want to take a
moment to break down

00:03:25.950 --> 00:03:29.790
service mesh infrastructure
and look at what

00:03:29.790 --> 00:03:32.050
we want out of that.

00:03:32.050 --> 00:03:35.100
And to do that, I want to just
take a step back and start

00:03:35.100 --> 00:03:36.480
from the basics.

00:03:36.480 --> 00:03:39.165
Now, some of you may be familiar
with Istio, maybe some of you

00:03:39.165 --> 00:03:40.920
are running Istio.

00:03:40.920 --> 00:03:42.670
Istio is in production.

00:03:42.670 --> 00:03:44.850
We've got some CVEs
even that were updated,

00:03:44.850 --> 00:03:47.370
so you've kind of updated
some vulnerabilities that

00:03:47.370 --> 00:03:48.990
were discovered recently.

00:03:48.990 --> 00:03:50.490
Maybe you're further
along the path,

00:03:50.490 --> 00:03:53.240
but maybe I suspect that a lot
of you are looking at Istio,

00:03:53.240 --> 00:03:55.560
are examining Services
Mesh infrastructure,

00:03:55.560 --> 00:03:59.370
are kind of building your own
Services Mesh infrastructure

00:03:59.370 --> 00:04:00.570
with different proxies.

00:04:00.570 --> 00:04:03.600
Maybe you're using Nginx,
maybe you're using HA Proxy,

00:04:03.600 --> 00:04:05.520
maybe you're using
Linkerd, maybe you're

00:04:05.520 --> 00:04:08.310
using Envoy at its
base, and you're

00:04:08.310 --> 00:04:11.490
less familiar with all of what's
going on in Istio or in Service

00:04:11.490 --> 00:04:12.400
Mesh in general.

00:04:12.400 --> 00:04:14.830
So let's kind of take a step
back and just look at that.

00:04:14.830 --> 00:04:16.830
So the first thing is
services intercommunicate.

00:04:16.830 --> 00:04:19.079
So we have a bunch of
them in the constellation

00:04:19.079 --> 00:04:19.980
intercommunicating.

00:04:19.980 --> 00:04:21.480
We're just going
to look at two just

00:04:21.480 --> 00:04:23.160
for the purposes of
illustration here.

00:04:23.160 --> 00:04:26.400
And we've got that API
that's connecting these two.

00:04:26.400 --> 00:04:31.320
Once we have disconnection or
decomposition of the monolith

00:04:31.320 --> 00:04:33.120
into multiple
different services,

00:04:33.120 --> 00:04:36.030
things that we had some
assumptions about before, which

00:04:36.030 --> 00:04:40.020
is, for example, a connection
between different modules

00:04:40.020 --> 00:04:42.630
is going to be in
process and secure,

00:04:42.630 --> 00:04:45.630
those assumptions are no
longer accurate, right?

00:04:45.630 --> 00:04:47.700
No longer applicable.

00:04:47.700 --> 00:04:50.760
Now, the different
modules are services,

00:04:50.760 --> 00:04:52.470
are realized
services, and there is

00:04:52.470 --> 00:04:53.950
remote communication
between them,

00:04:53.950 --> 00:04:55.150
so we need to secure that.

00:04:55.150 --> 00:04:57.152
So if you're going to
go into microservices,

00:04:57.152 --> 00:04:59.610
one of the very first things
that you want to be able to do

00:04:59.610 --> 00:05:03.780
is have mutual TLS so that
both peers in the exchange

00:05:03.780 --> 00:05:05.850
can authenticate
each other, and you

00:05:05.850 --> 00:05:09.600
can perform authorization
decisions at the service level

00:05:09.600 --> 00:05:12.720
based on the identity that's
been asserted with the TLS

00:05:12.720 --> 00:05:13.570
certificate.

00:05:13.570 --> 00:05:15.660
So this is all just
kind of basic stuff,

00:05:15.660 --> 00:05:17.850
but it's kind of hard to do.

00:05:17.850 --> 00:05:20.400
When you have just two
services, no problem.

00:05:20.400 --> 00:05:22.290
You can issue certs,
they can compare them,

00:05:22.290 --> 00:05:24.540
you can make sure this is
the peer that I expect,

00:05:24.540 --> 00:05:25.740
and everything's good.

00:05:25.740 --> 00:05:27.510
As you add a number
of services, that

00:05:27.510 --> 00:05:29.430
starts to get a little
more complicated.

00:05:29.430 --> 00:05:31.620
But for sure, you're
going to want that.

00:05:31.620 --> 00:05:35.090
Ideally, you want to
provision certs automatically.

00:05:35.090 --> 00:05:37.847
So remember, keep in your mind,
we're looking at just two,

00:05:37.847 --> 00:05:39.680
but there's a constellation
of these things.

00:05:39.680 --> 00:05:43.080
There's 15, there's 20
different cooperating services,

00:05:43.080 --> 00:05:45.190
and I want to provision
search for all of them.

00:05:45.190 --> 00:05:47.070
And I want them to be distinct.

00:05:47.070 --> 00:05:48.137
It's cheating.

00:05:48.137 --> 00:05:49.720
I know there's some
of you out there--

00:05:49.720 --> 00:05:52.140
it's cheating to use the
same cert for every service.

00:05:52.140 --> 00:05:53.580
You can't do that.

00:05:53.580 --> 00:05:55.413
That's not the way
it's going to work.

00:05:55.413 --> 00:05:56.580
It's not going to work well.

00:05:56.580 --> 00:05:58.900
So you need different
certs for each service,

00:05:58.900 --> 00:06:01.350
and you'd like to have
policies in place as

00:06:01.350 --> 00:06:03.990
well for these
inter-cooperating services.

00:06:03.990 --> 00:06:05.290
What used to be really easy?

00:06:05.290 --> 00:06:06.420
Just call the module.

00:06:06.420 --> 00:06:07.440
It's in process call.

00:06:07.440 --> 00:06:08.280
It's a method call.

00:06:08.280 --> 00:06:10.740
I'm calling to a
different object.

00:06:10.740 --> 00:06:15.120
What used to be super easy is
now maybe a little bit iffy.

00:06:15.120 --> 00:06:16.140
There's a network there.

00:06:16.140 --> 00:06:17.880
The network is not
always present.

00:06:17.880 --> 00:06:19.810
The instance is
not always there.

00:06:19.810 --> 00:06:23.100
So you need policies like, am
I going to retry this call?

00:06:23.100 --> 00:06:25.110
If I am going to
retry, how many times?

00:06:25.110 --> 00:06:27.160
What's the back off interval?

00:06:27.160 --> 00:06:28.200
What's the timeout?

00:06:28.200 --> 00:06:30.780
At what point does the client,
the initiator of the call,

00:06:30.780 --> 00:06:32.400
say this has failed.

00:06:32.400 --> 00:06:34.710
I'm going to declare
this call failed

00:06:34.710 --> 00:06:37.293
and maybe retry or
maybe just give up.

00:06:37.293 --> 00:06:38.460
How do I do circuit breaker?

00:06:38.460 --> 00:06:41.550
So if I continue to try
to hit the same instance

00:06:41.550 --> 00:06:43.050
and that thing is
just not giving me

00:06:43.050 --> 00:06:46.000
a satisfactory response, maybe
it's no connection at all

00:06:46.000 --> 00:06:47.910
but maybe it's
just an error, I'd

00:06:47.910 --> 00:06:49.890
like to kind of take
that out of rotation

00:06:49.890 --> 00:06:52.110
and not connect to
that instance anymore.

00:06:52.110 --> 00:06:54.540
If I'm doing Services Mesh,
I've got multiple instances.

00:06:54.540 --> 00:06:57.360
So I can connect
to different ones.

00:06:57.360 --> 00:06:59.940
But let me just take that one
out, just flip that circuit

00:06:59.940 --> 00:07:01.608
breaker and pull it out.

00:07:01.608 --> 00:07:03.150
I want to be able
to do routing based

00:07:03.150 --> 00:07:06.417
on maybe content or identity,
time of day, authorization.

00:07:06.417 --> 00:07:08.250
So all of those things
are sort of policies,

00:07:08.250 --> 00:07:09.480
and there may be more.

00:07:09.480 --> 00:07:11.850
And you kind of want to
implement all of those

00:07:11.850 --> 00:07:14.430
in the disparate services.

00:07:14.430 --> 00:07:16.350
And remember, each
of these services,

00:07:16.350 --> 00:07:18.540
we may be talking about
a polyglot environment.

00:07:18.540 --> 00:07:21.280
So while I am decomposing--
let's say I've got a Java app

00:07:21.280 --> 00:07:24.400
or I've got a .NET app, and
I'm decomposing the Java code

00:07:24.400 --> 00:07:26.470
or the C # code into
multiple different services.

00:07:26.470 --> 00:07:28.720
Then I can say, OK,
it's all one language.

00:07:28.720 --> 00:07:29.560
Super easy.

00:07:29.560 --> 00:07:32.560
But we all know that that's
not how it works in practice.

00:07:32.560 --> 00:07:34.780
Yes, you're going to
decompose an existing system,

00:07:34.780 --> 00:07:36.700
but then there's a new
team that's coming up

00:07:36.700 --> 00:07:38.140
and they want to use
a different language.

00:07:38.140 --> 00:07:40.600
They've got this Ruby module
that does just the right thing.

00:07:40.600 --> 00:07:42.100
They've got Python
that they want to try.

00:07:42.100 --> 00:07:44.050
They're much more
productive with that you're.

00:07:44.050 --> 00:07:46.420
Using Node.js, so
you can't have all

00:07:46.420 --> 00:07:50.590
of these provisions, all
these policies implemented

00:07:50.590 --> 00:07:53.440
in all those different
languages in the same way

00:07:53.440 --> 00:07:55.150
in a compatible way.

00:07:55.150 --> 00:07:58.030
So the thinking
here is that rather

00:07:58.030 --> 00:08:01.030
than build a library for
each one of those languages

00:08:01.030 --> 00:08:05.740
and platforms for Java, for C
#, for Python and so on, let's

00:08:05.740 --> 00:08:10.480
factor out all that capability,
all that smarts, the policy

00:08:10.480 --> 00:08:14.652
enforcement as I talked
about, the MTLS management,

00:08:14.652 --> 00:08:15.610
the session management.

00:08:15.610 --> 00:08:20.410
Maybe I've got requirements for
the particular algorithms that

00:08:20.410 --> 00:08:23.570
are used in the TLS connection.

00:08:23.570 --> 00:08:25.730
Let's factor that out
and put it in a proxy.

00:08:25.730 --> 00:08:27.850
And it's one proxy that
works with everybody,

00:08:27.850 --> 00:08:29.950
and we'll call it
a sidecar proxy.

00:08:29.950 --> 00:08:34.780
So this is the model that
works in a scalable fashion

00:08:34.780 --> 00:08:37.330
for Services Mesh
infrastructure.

00:08:37.330 --> 00:08:39.483
And in fact, this
is the Istio model.

00:08:39.483 --> 00:08:40.900
This is the model
that Istio uses.

00:08:40.900 --> 00:08:43.049
It uses that sidecar proxy.

00:08:43.049 --> 00:08:45.250
It's actually implemented
by a separate open source

00:08:45.250 --> 00:08:47.530
project called Envoy.

00:08:47.530 --> 00:08:50.590
All of that stuff,
the TLS management,

00:08:50.590 --> 00:08:53.620
the policy enforcement, all
of that is done by Envoy.

00:08:53.620 --> 00:08:57.460
And your service, you can
implement that in Java, .NET,

00:08:57.460 --> 00:09:00.730
Node.js, it connects only to
the outside world either with

00:09:00.730 --> 00:09:04.360
inbound or outbound
communication via that proxy,

00:09:04.360 --> 00:09:06.680
via that sidecar proxy.

00:09:06.680 --> 00:09:08.000
This is how Istio works.

00:09:08.000 --> 00:09:09.772
And Istio adds
that control plane

00:09:09.772 --> 00:09:11.230
that you see depicted
in the bottom

00:09:11.230 --> 00:09:13.605
here with a couple
of different modules

00:09:13.605 --> 00:09:15.730
that you can read about in
the Istio documentation.

00:09:15.730 --> 00:09:17.700
This isn't a deep
dive into Istio,

00:09:17.700 --> 00:09:21.340
but just kind of an overview
that allows certificates,

00:09:21.340 --> 00:09:24.940
for example, to be
provisioned for each instance,

00:09:24.940 --> 00:09:29.770
for each type of service in your
Mesh and authorization rules

00:09:29.770 --> 00:09:33.760
to be provisions centrally and
then sent out to the proxies

00:09:33.760 --> 00:09:35.420
so that they can enforce.

00:09:35.420 --> 00:09:37.860
If I'm service B, I'm
going to allow only service

00:09:37.860 --> 00:09:39.080
A to call me.

00:09:39.080 --> 00:09:41.140
I'm not going to allow
service C to call me

00:09:41.140 --> 00:09:43.990
or any other new service that
gets introduced into the Mesh

00:09:43.990 --> 00:09:46.340
if you choose to
restrict it that way.

00:09:46.340 --> 00:09:48.640
So those are the kinds of
things that the control plane

00:09:48.640 --> 00:09:52.420
for Istio do in this mechanism.

00:09:52.420 --> 00:09:54.640
You'll see those two
different diagrams, data flow

00:09:54.640 --> 00:09:56.080
and control metrics flow.

00:09:56.080 --> 00:09:59.427
The high throughput,
low latency calls,

00:09:59.427 --> 00:10:01.510
the things that are kind
of running your business,

00:10:01.510 --> 00:10:03.010
are all happening
in the data plane.

00:10:03.010 --> 00:10:04.450
One service talking
to the other,

00:10:04.450 --> 00:10:07.330
lots of services kind
of connecting together.

00:10:07.330 --> 00:10:10.000
You have this Mesh of
services all inter-cooperating

00:10:10.000 --> 00:10:11.050
at very high speed.

00:10:11.050 --> 00:10:12.675
That's all happening
in the data plane.

00:10:12.675 --> 00:10:14.200
The control plane
is just separate.

00:10:14.200 --> 00:10:17.200
It does not get invoked
for every communication,

00:10:17.200 --> 00:10:18.550
for every request.

00:10:18.550 --> 00:10:19.570
Most of that is cached.

00:10:19.570 --> 00:10:20.495
If you want to
make a change, you

00:10:20.495 --> 00:10:22.390
make it in the control plane,
and then it gets provisioned.

00:10:22.390 --> 00:10:24.220
The proxies get
updated, and they

00:10:24.220 --> 00:10:27.130
behave according to your
configuration on the next go

00:10:27.130 --> 00:10:28.060
round.

00:10:28.060 --> 00:10:29.800
So Istio also adds
some other things

00:10:29.800 --> 00:10:31.800
you're probably going to
want as a Service Mesh,

00:10:31.800 --> 00:10:33.198
and that is an Ingress Gateway.

00:10:33.198 --> 00:10:34.990
So now I've got this
Mesh, and I'm managing

00:10:34.990 --> 00:10:36.550
all the communications between.

00:10:36.550 --> 00:10:40.360
I've got TLS security, so
I'm encrypting all the point

00:10:40.360 --> 00:10:41.770
to point communications.

00:10:41.770 --> 00:10:43.240
I've got policy enforcement.

00:10:43.240 --> 00:10:45.160
I'm retrying the way I want.

00:10:45.160 --> 00:10:46.730
I've got back off intervals.

00:10:46.730 --> 00:10:48.040
I've got circuit breakers.

00:10:48.040 --> 00:10:50.380
But I also want to admit
inbound communications

00:10:50.380 --> 00:10:53.830
to that controlled Mesh, to the
thing that I'm managing nicely.

00:10:53.830 --> 00:10:55.390
I want to have an
Ingress Gateway,

00:10:55.390 --> 00:10:58.032
so Istio adds this as
well, adds this concept.

00:10:58.032 --> 00:10:59.740
And what it allows is
an internal client,

00:10:59.740 --> 00:11:02.290
some other client application
that's not part of the Mesh

00:11:02.290 --> 00:11:03.590
to send in requests.

00:11:03.590 --> 00:11:06.070
And again, you can have
policies there too that allow--

00:11:06.070 --> 00:11:09.760
you can say it needs to bear
a JWT, it needs to bare a Jot,

00:11:09.760 --> 00:11:11.610
and it has to have
this kind of issuer.

00:11:11.610 --> 00:11:14.920
Or it needs to have TLS, or
it needs to have mutual TLS,

00:11:14.920 --> 00:11:17.320
and the TLS CN has
to be such and such.

00:11:17.320 --> 00:11:21.320
So you can do all that with
the Ingress Gateway as well.

00:11:21.320 --> 00:11:22.870
Likewise, you're
going to want to be

00:11:22.870 --> 00:11:24.287
able to control
communication that

00:11:24.287 --> 00:11:30.100
is initiated from inside the
Services Mesh and sent outside.

00:11:30.100 --> 00:11:33.693
In many environments, you
just don't care about that.

00:11:33.693 --> 00:11:35.860
You've got network firewall
rules running somewhere.

00:11:35.860 --> 00:11:39.293
But in some, you want to be
even more careful about that.

00:11:39.293 --> 00:11:41.210
And you may want to do
some routing decisions.

00:11:41.210 --> 00:11:44.710
So there's a way to manage
outbound communications

00:11:44.710 --> 00:11:45.650
as well.

00:11:45.650 --> 00:11:48.940
And this is what we would call
a Managed Mesh of services,

00:11:48.940 --> 00:11:50.883
or a Service Mesh.

00:11:50.883 --> 00:11:52.300
Some people say--
and I want to be

00:11:52.300 --> 00:11:55.065
particular about the definition
of the term Service Mesh.

00:11:55.065 --> 00:11:56.440
Some people think
of Service Mesh

00:11:56.440 --> 00:11:57.982
as you know, Istio
as a Service Mesh.

00:11:57.982 --> 00:11:59.982
And there may be other
tools that are out there,

00:11:59.982 --> 00:12:02.477
other pieces of infrastructure
that are out there that you

00:12:02.477 --> 00:12:03.310
call a Service Mesh.

00:12:03.310 --> 00:12:06.180
The way I think about
it is a Service Mesh

00:12:06.180 --> 00:12:07.950
is a mesh of services.

00:12:07.950 --> 00:12:10.340
It's just a bunch of services
that are inter-cooperating

00:12:10.340 --> 00:12:11.820
and intercommunicating.

00:12:11.820 --> 00:12:13.710
Service Mesh
Infrastructure is what

00:12:13.710 --> 00:12:15.370
allows you to manage all that.

00:12:15.370 --> 00:12:17.970
So that's just my
kind of phrasing,

00:12:17.970 --> 00:12:20.760
but you'll hear people
throughout the conference say,

00:12:20.760 --> 00:12:22.350
Istio is a Service Mesh.

00:12:22.350 --> 00:12:25.260
To me, that feels just
a little bit inaccurate,

00:12:25.260 --> 00:12:27.270
but I get what they're saying.

00:12:27.270 --> 00:12:30.300
In the rest of this talk, we'll
be talking about Service Mesh

00:12:30.300 --> 00:12:34.240
Infrastructure, and Istio
is a good example of that.

00:12:34.240 --> 00:12:39.990
OK, so that's kind of a brief,
quick overview of Service Mesh,

00:12:39.990 --> 00:12:42.480
and this is why you would
want to use a Service Mesh.

00:12:42.480 --> 00:12:45.240
It's connecting, securing,
controlling, and observing

00:12:45.240 --> 00:12:47.960
the services in that mesh.

00:12:47.960 --> 00:12:50.240
Next thing I want to talk
about is API management,

00:12:50.240 --> 00:12:52.610
and it's management
for shared APIs.

00:12:52.610 --> 00:12:54.710
So shared APIs may be
wrapped around legacy

00:12:54.710 --> 00:12:57.050
systems, monoliths, and what
we want to be able to do

00:12:57.050 --> 00:13:00.440
is allow external clients
to call into those systems.

00:13:00.440 --> 00:13:01.520
Why you use management?

00:13:01.520 --> 00:13:02.930
First, discovery.

00:13:02.930 --> 00:13:05.480
So an external
developer, we want

00:13:05.480 --> 00:13:08.380
to allow those external
developers to see what APIs are

00:13:08.380 --> 00:13:10.550
available and then provision
credentials for use

00:13:10.550 --> 00:13:12.020
by that developer
in the apps they

00:13:12.020 --> 00:13:15.500
build in order to make
calls into the API Gateway.

00:13:15.500 --> 00:13:17.210
We want to modernize systems.

00:13:17.210 --> 00:13:19.190
We want to report,
and we potentially

00:13:19.190 --> 00:13:21.410
want to monetize APIs.

00:13:21.410 --> 00:13:23.913
On modernization specifically,
we'll talk about a couple

00:13:23.913 --> 00:13:25.080
of different scenarios here.

00:13:25.080 --> 00:13:26.995
Maybe you've got
a SOAP back end,

00:13:26.995 --> 00:13:28.370
maybe you've got
an XML back end,

00:13:28.370 --> 00:13:30.830
you want to kind of
make it look like JSON.

00:13:30.830 --> 00:13:35.120
Or maybe want to add caching,
you may want to add routing.

00:13:35.120 --> 00:13:38.450
And modernization,
this aspect helps a lot

00:13:38.450 --> 00:13:40.460
when you're breaking down
the monolith as we'll

00:13:40.460 --> 00:13:41.630
see a little bit later.

00:13:41.630 --> 00:13:44.300
You can add routing right
in that API Gateway layer

00:13:44.300 --> 00:13:46.790
pointing some calls to the
existing monolith and some

00:13:46.790 --> 00:13:50.780
to the new microservices.

00:13:50.780 --> 00:13:54.050
So this is what it might
look like as an evolution

00:13:54.050 --> 00:13:59.030
of combining Services Mesh with
API management and API Gateway.

00:13:59.030 --> 00:14:01.452
So we've got our API
Gateway, external clients

00:14:01.452 --> 00:14:03.410
calling through there,
internal clients calling

00:14:03.410 --> 00:14:05.407
the systems that
exist, and we start

00:14:05.407 --> 00:14:06.740
to introduce some microservices.

00:14:06.740 --> 00:14:09.067
Maybe we're on that journey,
decomposing the monolith

00:14:09.067 --> 00:14:11.150
into different microservices,
and then maybe we're

00:14:11.150 --> 00:14:12.380
adding a few services.

00:14:12.380 --> 00:14:15.930
It's not just decomposing,
but it's adding a few things.

00:14:15.930 --> 00:14:18.740
It's augmenting where we're
modernizing and adding

00:14:18.740 --> 00:14:21.457
and extending, and so
now the internal app

00:14:21.457 --> 00:14:23.540
is going to be calling
into the existing monolith,

00:14:23.540 --> 00:14:26.180
the new services, and
the API Gateway can

00:14:26.180 --> 00:14:29.607
kind of direct and route
calls in as we see fit.

00:14:29.607 --> 00:14:31.190
We start to see
proliferation, though,

00:14:31.190 --> 00:14:32.690
and this is when
you're really going

00:14:32.690 --> 00:14:35.300
to benefit from that
Services Mesh Infrastructure.

00:14:35.300 --> 00:14:36.920
Something like Istio,
and that's when

00:14:36.920 --> 00:14:40.430
you can create these clusters,
these clusters of services,

00:14:40.430 --> 00:14:43.070
and establish those policies,
all the things that I

00:14:43.070 --> 00:14:45.820
talked about in the
beginning of this session.

00:14:45.820 --> 00:14:48.182
You can have multiple
different clusters, obviously.

00:14:48.182 --> 00:14:49.640
They can run in
the same Kubernetes

00:14:49.640 --> 00:14:51.260
cluster or different.

00:14:51.260 --> 00:14:54.140
You can run across clouds,
and Greg will actually

00:14:54.140 --> 00:14:57.990
show you a little bit of
multi cluster scenarios.

00:14:57.990 --> 00:15:02.270
This is what we think of
as the kind of clean model

00:15:02.270 --> 00:15:06.380
that composes API management
and Service Mesh Management,

00:15:06.380 --> 00:15:09.330
or Service Mesh Infrastructure
in an environment.

00:15:09.330 --> 00:15:12.577
So this is a clean
picture, super easy.

00:15:12.577 --> 00:15:14.160
A person came up to
me and said, look,

00:15:14.160 --> 00:15:16.280
I have a question about
maybe internal clients.

00:15:16.280 --> 00:15:18.655
Do internal clients if they
want to connect to my system,

00:15:18.655 --> 00:15:22.220
do they have to go out and
into the API Gateway just

00:15:22.220 --> 00:15:23.190
to get into the system?

00:15:23.190 --> 00:15:24.398
And the answer to that is no.

00:15:24.398 --> 00:15:26.690
We already talked about
the Ingress Gateway, that's

00:15:26.690 --> 00:15:29.232
part of Istio, and you're going
to have that with any Service

00:15:29.232 --> 00:15:30.410
Mesh Infrastructure.

00:15:30.410 --> 00:15:32.330
So internal clients
can continue to use

00:15:32.330 --> 00:15:34.820
those kinds of approaches,
but external clients

00:15:34.820 --> 00:15:37.880
for reasons we'll get
into will probably

00:15:37.880 --> 00:15:41.990
want to use something maybe a
little more structured in order

00:15:41.990 --> 00:15:45.160
to manage that
communication there.

00:15:45.160 --> 00:15:49.240
This is the mental model
that I like to offer people

00:15:49.240 --> 00:15:53.920
when they're thinking about
how services and APIs work

00:15:53.920 --> 00:15:55.090
in an enterprise.

00:15:55.090 --> 00:15:57.760
So at the base level, you
have your infrastructure.

00:15:57.760 --> 00:16:00.550
That's going to be your
platform as a service, your VMs,

00:16:00.550 --> 00:16:02.260
maybe it's Kubernetes
that you've chosen,

00:16:02.260 --> 00:16:04.677
maybe it's a composite
of those things.

00:16:04.677 --> 00:16:06.760
You've got different
projects and different people

00:16:06.760 --> 00:16:09.062
are using Cloud
Foundry, and who knows.

00:16:09.062 --> 00:16:11.020
You've got a bunch of
different infrastructure.

00:16:11.020 --> 00:16:14.583
On top of that, in some way,
you're building services.

00:16:14.583 --> 00:16:16.000
And what we like
to say is there's

00:16:16.000 --> 00:16:17.650
going to be thousands
of those depending

00:16:17.650 --> 00:16:20.067
on the size of your company,
and you want to manage those.

00:16:20.067 --> 00:16:22.390
You want to wrap services
management around those.

00:16:22.390 --> 00:16:26.170
Now, not all services
are going to be managed.

00:16:26.170 --> 00:16:31.060
It's an aspirational goal
to have all of them managed.

00:16:31.060 --> 00:16:33.300
Exposed by those
services are APIs.

00:16:33.300 --> 00:16:37.390
And in the same way we aspire to
have the thousands of services

00:16:37.390 --> 00:16:41.050
be under management,
we aspire to have

00:16:41.050 --> 00:16:44.115
the APIs that get shared
out of those services--

00:16:44.115 --> 00:16:46.240
not the ones that they're
using to intercommunicate

00:16:46.240 --> 00:16:48.430
within the mesh, but the
ones that get shared out--

00:16:48.430 --> 00:16:50.620
we aspire to have those
under management too.

00:16:50.620 --> 00:16:51.897
Not all of them will be.

00:16:51.897 --> 00:16:53.980
There are some pitfalls
associated to not managing

00:16:53.980 --> 00:16:56.960
your APIs, and Greg and I
can both attest to that,

00:16:56.960 --> 00:16:58.480
but that's the aspiration.

00:16:58.480 --> 00:17:00.070
So that's the model.

00:17:00.070 --> 00:17:02.050
Platform, thousands of services.

00:17:02.050 --> 00:17:04.599
Hundreds of those may be
exposing shared APIs that

00:17:04.599 --> 00:17:08.270
get consumed by clients that
are either inside the company

00:17:08.270 --> 00:17:10.810
but distant from the
teams that are producing

00:17:10.810 --> 00:17:14.349
or outside of the company,
some external client.

00:17:14.349 --> 00:17:16.690
And that's the mental model.

00:17:16.690 --> 00:17:19.630
Now with that, I'm going
to hand it over to Greg,

00:17:19.630 --> 00:17:23.904
and he's going to make this real
for us with a demonstration.

00:17:23.904 --> 00:17:25.450
GREG KUELGEN: Awesome.

00:17:25.450 --> 00:17:26.740
Thank you, Dino.

00:17:26.740 --> 00:17:29.560
All right, so I'm going to
go through this pretty quick

00:17:29.560 --> 00:17:31.122
in the interest of
time, but I want

00:17:31.122 --> 00:17:32.830
to set up with a little
bit of background

00:17:32.830 --> 00:17:35.247
in terms of what you're going
to see in the demonstration.

00:17:35.247 --> 00:17:37.990
Of course, we're going to use
the proverbial Acme Company.

00:17:37.990 --> 00:17:39.070
What haven't they done?

00:17:39.070 --> 00:17:40.570
They're a pretty
typical enterprise.

00:17:40.570 --> 00:17:41.987
They've got different
departments,

00:17:41.987 --> 00:17:43.112
divisions, locations.

00:17:43.112 --> 00:17:45.070
Today, we're going to
concentrate on two teams.

00:17:45.070 --> 00:17:47.545
We're going to concentrate
on the Product Team, who

00:17:47.545 --> 00:17:49.840
manage things like
products, product catalog,

00:17:49.840 --> 00:17:50.830
and the Logistics Team.

00:17:50.830 --> 00:17:52.390
They manage the
warehouse, delivery,

00:17:52.390 --> 00:17:54.530
those sorts of things.

00:17:54.530 --> 00:17:56.320
Now, Acme's feeling
a lot of pressure

00:17:56.320 --> 00:17:59.740
from startups kind of trying
to disrupt the market,

00:17:59.740 --> 00:18:02.070
and the business has
asked IT to move faster.

00:18:02.070 --> 00:18:04.570
So both the Product Team and
the Logistics Team took a look,

00:18:04.570 --> 00:18:06.792
and they said, you know what?

00:18:06.792 --> 00:18:09.250
Maybe if we modernize a couple
of these existing monoliths,

00:18:09.250 --> 00:18:10.750
we can move faster.

00:18:10.750 --> 00:18:11.980
And they made some decisions.

00:18:11.980 --> 00:18:14.050
They chose to go
with a Kubernetes

00:18:14.050 --> 00:18:17.830
based system using Service
Mesh Infrastructure and API

00:18:17.830 --> 00:18:19.780
management.

00:18:19.780 --> 00:18:22.120
The Product Team
has three services

00:18:22.120 --> 00:18:23.313
that we're looking at here.

00:18:23.313 --> 00:18:24.980
They have their product
service and then

00:18:24.980 --> 00:18:26.860
a location and a
pricing service.

00:18:26.860 --> 00:18:29.560
The main consumer of the
product service is a mobile app.

00:18:29.560 --> 00:18:31.300
It's an internally
developed mobile app.

00:18:31.300 --> 00:18:33.592
You'll notice in this case,
that mobile app is actually

00:18:33.592 --> 00:18:35.650
going through an
external API gateway,

00:18:35.650 --> 00:18:38.170
in this case, the Apigee API
Gateway, that happens to be

00:18:38.170 --> 00:18:41.590
running as Sas in the cloud.

00:18:41.590 --> 00:18:45.285
The logistics team has
an inventory service.

00:18:45.285 --> 00:18:46.660
They also have a
third party that

00:18:46.660 --> 00:18:49.035
accesses that inventory service
that helps them and needs

00:18:49.035 --> 00:18:51.070
accurate inventory counts.

00:18:51.070 --> 00:18:53.640
So with that, let's get
started with the demo.

00:18:53.640 --> 00:18:54.890
Can we go to the demo, please?

00:18:59.540 --> 00:19:02.945
All right, so we talked
about the Product Team.

00:19:02.945 --> 00:19:04.320
That's what we're
going to start.

00:19:04.320 --> 00:19:06.590
They are using
Kubernetes, and they

00:19:06.590 --> 00:19:09.810
made what is the obvious choice
for where to run Kubernetes,

00:19:09.810 --> 00:19:12.950
which is GKE.

00:19:12.950 --> 00:19:15.350
So they've got a few
different clusters here.

00:19:15.350 --> 00:19:16.670
The cluster that we're
going to worry about today

00:19:16.670 --> 00:19:17.840
is this dual cluster one.

00:19:17.840 --> 00:19:21.215
It's a four node cluster,
and let's just take a look

00:19:21.215 --> 00:19:23.340
and see what they've actually
got running in there.

00:19:27.180 --> 00:19:29.430
So you'll see just like
what we would expect to see,

00:19:29.430 --> 00:19:32.240
we've got a product service, a
pricing service, and a location

00:19:32.240 --> 00:19:32.740
service.

00:19:32.740 --> 00:19:34.907
Those are the pods that are
running in this cluster.

00:19:37.200 --> 00:19:40.680
So at first, they kind
of built these services,

00:19:40.680 --> 00:19:41.853
and everything was great.

00:19:41.853 --> 00:19:43.270
And then they the
Operations Team,

00:19:43.270 --> 00:19:45.970
the Security Team come along and
start asking questions about,

00:19:45.970 --> 00:19:48.000
hey, which service
communicates with which?

00:19:48.000 --> 00:19:48.960
How do I view the logs?

00:19:48.960 --> 00:19:50.200
All of that stuff.

00:19:50.200 --> 00:19:52.600
So they decided that
they're developers,

00:19:52.600 --> 00:19:54.952
they could develop libraries
and build all this stuff

00:19:54.952 --> 00:19:57.160
into their services, but
they didn't want to do that.

00:19:57.160 --> 00:19:59.910
So they chose to use Istio
as their Service Mesh

00:19:59.910 --> 00:20:02.825
Infrastructure to handle
all those things for them.

00:20:02.825 --> 00:20:04.200
So let's just take
a look and see

00:20:04.200 --> 00:20:06.948
what they've got running from
an Istio perspective in here.

00:20:06.948 --> 00:20:08.490
And you'll forgive
me, I'm just going

00:20:08.490 --> 00:20:11.160
to copy all my commands
because otherwise, you'll

00:20:11.160 --> 00:20:15.390
see lots of ID10T errors
as I try to type in.

00:20:15.390 --> 00:20:17.010
And if you don't
know ID10T is, you

00:20:17.010 --> 00:20:19.722
can type that into your phone
and figure out what that is.

00:20:19.722 --> 00:20:21.180
So you'll see here,
there's a bunch

00:20:21.180 --> 00:20:25.170
of pods running for Istio
that enable the Service Mesh.

00:20:25.170 --> 00:20:27.690
You'll see things like
Citadel and Citadel Pilot

00:20:27.690 --> 00:20:30.750
that you also saw up on
Dino's diagrams up there.

00:20:34.060 --> 00:20:36.260
And so now what I
actually want to do is--

00:20:36.260 --> 00:20:39.280
I'm going to go on a small
tangent very briefly.

00:20:39.280 --> 00:20:45.020
So previously in my career, I
worked for a logistics company.

00:20:45.020 --> 00:20:46.930
And one of the things
I was responsible for

00:20:46.930 --> 00:20:48.790
was handling all
external communications

00:20:48.790 --> 00:20:51.980
with third parties,
customers, what have you.

00:20:51.980 --> 00:20:53.560
Now occasionally,
we'd have to set up

00:20:53.560 --> 00:20:56.680
a mutual TLS connection with
one of these third parties.

00:20:56.680 --> 00:20:59.800
That was pretty much always
my option of last resort.

00:20:59.800 --> 00:21:00.760
I hated it.

00:21:00.760 --> 00:21:02.650
And it usually went
something like this.

00:21:02.650 --> 00:21:04.337
So we'd get on a call,
and sometimes I'd

00:21:04.337 --> 00:21:05.920
have to explain to
them why I wouldn't

00:21:05.920 --> 00:21:07.420
create a private cert for them.

00:21:07.420 --> 00:21:10.450
And then we'd exchange cets,
and I'd load the key on my side,

00:21:10.450 --> 00:21:11.980
and they'd send something in.

00:21:11.980 --> 00:21:14.320
And they'd say,
it's not working.

00:21:14.320 --> 00:21:16.397
And I'd say, well,
you sent me y,

00:21:16.397 --> 00:21:18.730
but I see you're actually
sending me x when you send it,

00:21:18.730 --> 00:21:19.630
and they'll say, no, no, no.

00:21:19.630 --> 00:21:20.230
We're sending y.

00:21:20.230 --> 00:21:21.280
There's no way we're sending x.

00:21:21.280 --> 00:21:23.380
And that would go on from
anywhere between an hour

00:21:23.380 --> 00:21:25.040
to two weeks.

00:21:25.040 --> 00:21:29.170
So now, I want to enable that
same feature within Istio

00:21:29.170 --> 00:21:31.647
for our different components.

00:21:31.647 --> 00:21:32.980
Just a little bit of setup here.

00:21:32.980 --> 00:21:35.920
I've got another container that
is sitting in a namespace that

00:21:35.920 --> 00:21:37.608
is not Istio injection enabled.

00:21:37.608 --> 00:21:39.400
So I'm going to connect
into that container

00:21:39.400 --> 00:21:41.860
just to show you how it
calls the pricing service.

00:21:49.030 --> 00:21:50.590
All right, I'm in
that container.

00:21:50.590 --> 00:21:53.850
And I'm going to make a
call to our pricing service,

00:21:53.850 --> 00:21:55.780
and you'll see
actually it worked.

00:21:55.780 --> 00:21:56.943
So that's interesting.

00:21:56.943 --> 00:21:59.110
This isn't participating
in the mesh, but it worked.

00:21:59.110 --> 00:22:02.830
Well, right now we're in what
we call Permissive MTLS state.

00:22:02.830 --> 00:22:05.080
And what that is is, as
you're first bringing services

00:22:05.080 --> 00:22:06.642
