00:32:53.450 --> 00:32:56.900
Service Mesh, but through
the dual control plane setup,

00:32:56.900 --> 00:32:58.800
it's actually acting
as one Service Mesh.

00:32:58.800 --> 00:33:00.020
And then there's
a third party that

00:33:00.020 --> 00:33:01.460
again, calls to that
inventory service,

00:33:01.460 --> 00:33:03.043
but it doesn't go
through the gateway.

00:33:03.043 --> 00:33:04.850
It uses the Apigee
Istio adapter.

00:33:04.850 --> 00:33:07.010
With that, I'm going to
call Dino back up on stage,

00:33:07.010 --> 00:33:09.690
because I've got some
questions for him.

00:33:09.690 --> 00:33:13.740
So my first question is,
when do I need Service Mesh

00:33:13.740 --> 00:33:14.820
Infrastructure?

00:33:14.820 --> 00:33:16.710
DINO CHIESA: So
my opinion on this

00:33:16.710 --> 00:33:18.540
is anytime you have
a significant number

00:33:18.540 --> 00:33:22.200
of cooperating microservices,
get a Service Mesh

00:33:22.200 --> 00:33:24.955
Infrastructure in place,
because you want that MTLS--

00:33:24.955 --> 00:33:26.830
I thought you were going
to throw this to me.

00:33:26.830 --> 00:33:28.070
GREG KUELGEN: I was
thinking about it.

00:33:28.070 --> 00:33:28.740
I was going to throw it at you.

00:33:28.740 --> 00:33:31.420
DINO CHIESA: I was a little
scared there for a minute.

00:33:31.420 --> 00:33:32.970
You want that mutual TLS.

00:33:32.970 --> 00:33:34.970
You want to be able to
secure the communications

00:33:34.970 --> 00:33:38.310
between the two peers or
many, many different peers.

00:33:38.310 --> 00:33:40.030
And that becomes
really important.

00:33:40.030 --> 00:33:41.835
And if you get Istio,
then you get a bunch

00:33:41.835 --> 00:33:43.710
of other things for
free-- the observability,

00:33:43.710 --> 00:33:46.210
the aggregated logs,
and some other things.

00:33:46.210 --> 00:33:48.090
GREG KUELGEN: OK,
well that's fine.

00:33:48.090 --> 00:33:51.390
But my consumers, they need
a response pretty quickly.

00:33:51.390 --> 00:33:52.420
What about latency?

00:33:52.420 --> 00:33:53.580
DINO CHIESA: It's magic.

00:33:53.580 --> 00:33:55.260
There is no additional latency.

00:33:55.260 --> 00:33:55.950
GREG KUELGEN: Oh,
that's awesome.

00:33:55.950 --> 00:33:57.242
You heard it here first, folks.

00:33:57.242 --> 00:33:58.470
No additional latency.

00:33:58.470 --> 00:34:00.120
DINO CHIESA: Actually, OK.

00:34:00.120 --> 00:34:02.435
Let me just correct that,
just a little adjustment.

00:34:02.435 --> 00:34:03.810
There is a little
bit of latency.

00:34:03.810 --> 00:34:05.910
Small, but you're not
going to notice it.

00:34:05.910 --> 00:34:07.500
So small, invisible.

00:34:07.500 --> 00:34:08.500
GREG KUELGEN: Invisible.

00:34:08.500 --> 00:34:11.370
DINO CHIESA: No, seriously,
there will be some latency.

00:34:11.370 --> 00:34:12.273
There's an extra hop.

00:34:12.273 --> 00:34:13.190
There's a proxy there.

00:34:13.190 --> 00:34:15.239
You guys all saw
it in the picture,

00:34:15.239 --> 00:34:17.830
so there is going
to be a hop there.

00:34:17.830 --> 00:34:20.610
But it's a sidecar,
so that proxy

00:34:20.610 --> 00:34:23.070
resides in the same
pod with the service.

00:34:23.070 --> 00:34:27.780
So the hop to the proxy is
going to be relatively quick.

00:34:27.780 --> 00:34:30.120
The hop that the proxy makes
to the other proxy, that's

00:34:30.120 --> 00:34:32.078
the same hop that was
there in the first place.

00:34:32.078 --> 00:34:35.100
So the only change
is really the in pod

00:34:35.100 --> 00:34:38.210
hops that go from proxy to
service and service to proxy,

00:34:38.210 --> 00:34:44.610
and that we think is going to
be small, like sub millisecond.

00:34:44.610 --> 00:34:46.373
It depends, obviously, on load.

00:34:46.373 --> 00:34:47.790
But the question
you've got to ask

00:34:47.790 --> 00:34:50.760
is, is the latency
cost, which is real,

00:34:50.760 --> 00:34:52.139
worth the benefits that you get?

00:34:52.139 --> 00:34:54.900
And we think it is.

00:34:54.900 --> 00:34:57.240
GREG KUELGEN: OK, so is the
takeaway from this, then,

00:34:57.240 --> 00:35:00.210
that if there's a mobile
client, then use API Management?

00:35:00.210 --> 00:35:02.070
DINO CHIESA: Almost always.

00:35:02.070 --> 00:35:03.865
That's almost always the case.

00:35:03.865 --> 00:35:05.490
Now, if you have
mobile clients, that's

00:35:05.490 --> 00:35:08.300
not the only reason
to use API Management,

00:35:08.300 --> 00:35:11.250
but mobile clients in
particular introduce factors

00:35:11.250 --> 00:35:14.850
that you're going to want
API Management to handle.

00:35:14.850 --> 00:35:18.810
The perimeter security that is
required for mobile clients,

00:35:18.810 --> 00:35:24.270
it's beyond just TLS
enforcement or JWT verification.

00:35:24.270 --> 00:35:31.050
You might want to do things
like malicious content

00:35:31.050 --> 00:35:35.460
filtering or XML bomb
detection or adaptive

00:35:35.460 --> 00:35:42.360
rate limiting or traffic
pattern analysis, and maybe even

00:35:42.360 --> 00:35:44.850
token binding, where if
you get an Oath token,

00:35:44.850 --> 00:35:47.460
you can bind it to a
particular TLS cert.

00:35:47.460 --> 00:35:49.830
So lots of things on the
perimeter, API Management

00:35:49.830 --> 00:35:51.726
makes a lot of sense for that.

00:35:51.726 --> 00:35:53.600
GREG KUELGEN: Perfect.

00:35:53.600 --> 00:35:56.870
So then, what I'm hearing
is any external client

00:35:56.870 --> 00:35:58.160
uses API Management.

00:35:58.160 --> 00:35:59.823
DINO CHIESA: Yeah, probably.

00:35:59.823 --> 00:36:01.240
If you have external
clients, that

00:36:01.240 --> 00:36:03.020
means traffic is
coming from outside.

00:36:03.020 --> 00:36:05.340
Those clients are going to
be outside of your control.

00:36:05.340 --> 00:36:06.965
So even if it's a
client that let's say

00:36:06.965 --> 00:36:10.140
you built as a company and
you're giving it to consumers,

00:36:10.140 --> 00:36:11.912
it's running on
consumer mobile apps,

00:36:11.912 --> 00:36:14.120
as soon as you do that,
there's going to be a hacker.

00:36:14.120 --> 00:36:16.040
There's going to be a bunch of
hackers that kind of decompile

00:36:16.040 --> 00:36:18.080
that thing and figure
out what's going on

00:36:18.080 --> 00:36:20.390
and figure out the reverse
engineer of the API.

00:36:20.390 --> 00:36:22.700
So they're going to be
sending in APIs that look just

00:36:22.700 --> 00:36:26.360
like bona fide APIs, which is
why you want that perimeter

00:36:26.360 --> 00:36:27.020
security.

00:36:27.020 --> 00:36:28.320
So almost always.

00:36:28.320 --> 00:36:30.190
GREG KUELGEN: OK.

00:36:30.190 --> 00:36:33.950
Does API Management make sense
of all my clients are internal?

00:36:33.950 --> 00:36:38.890
DINO CHIESA: So if they're
all internal, probably not.

00:36:38.890 --> 00:36:42.700
But I think that that
scenario that you just raised

00:36:42.700 --> 00:36:43.970
seems to be unlikely.

00:36:43.970 --> 00:36:46.090
So the trend is towards
more and more connection,

00:36:46.090 --> 00:36:48.370
more and more clients.

00:36:48.370 --> 00:36:51.580
Any entity, any company,
any organization

00:36:51.580 --> 00:36:56.000
that is digitally isolated
is not going to thrive.

00:36:56.000 --> 00:36:59.800
So there's got to be some desire
to have inbound communications.

00:36:59.800 --> 00:37:04.360
Now, a project may be serving
only internal clients,

00:37:04.360 --> 00:37:06.490
and in that case,
you probably don't

00:37:06.490 --> 00:37:08.030
need API Management for that.

00:37:08.030 --> 00:37:13.210
But when you have external
clients, it sure makes sense.

00:37:13.210 --> 00:37:16.750
GREG KUELGEN: And before I
get to my next question, one

00:37:16.750 --> 00:37:19.870
of the things I like to say with
that is that your internal API

00:37:19.870 --> 00:37:21.947
today is your
external API tomorrow.

00:37:21.947 --> 00:37:23.030
You just don't realize it.

00:37:23.030 --> 00:37:25.280
And I've seen that over and
over again with customers.

00:37:25.280 --> 00:37:28.960
DINO CHIESA: A company builds
an API, it gets a lot of use,

00:37:28.960 --> 00:37:31.120
and somebody, some product
manager or executive

00:37:31.120 --> 00:37:32.180
says, you know what?

00:37:32.180 --> 00:37:34.180
We should extend that to
this partner over here.

00:37:34.180 --> 00:37:35.170
It is now external.

00:37:35.170 --> 00:37:37.725
So you're going to rapidly
need API Management there.

00:37:37.725 --> 00:37:40.270
GREG KUELGEN: So my next
question is kind of like,

00:37:40.270 --> 00:37:43.372
don't I get API Management with
Service Mesh Infrastructure?

00:37:43.372 --> 00:37:44.080
DINO CHIESA: Yes.

00:37:44.080 --> 00:37:48.670
You get some of the features of
API Management in the Service

00:37:48.670 --> 00:37:49.930
Mesh Infrastructure.

00:37:49.930 --> 00:37:53.440
So you get authentication,
you get the TLS capability,

00:37:53.440 --> 00:37:55.890
you get observation,
access control.

00:37:55.890 --> 00:37:59.170
But as we said, in particular
in the things that you showed,

00:37:59.170 --> 00:38:03.820
you want to self service
enable developers to go figure

00:38:03.820 --> 00:38:06.880
out what APIs are available,
get credentials and embed

00:38:06.880 --> 00:38:08.415
those credentials
into their apps.

00:38:08.415 --> 00:38:10.165
So it's not always
going to be an API key.

00:38:10.165 --> 00:38:11.957
It might be a different
set of credentials.

00:38:11.957 --> 00:38:13.660
It might be a public
private key pair

00:38:13.660 --> 00:38:15.330
that you're issuing
as credentials.

00:38:15.330 --> 00:38:17.080
But you want to have
that be self-service.

00:38:17.080 --> 00:38:19.340
You don't want to have people
e-mailing around saying, hey,

00:38:19.340 --> 00:38:21.757
I need credentials, and here
we're going to send an email.

00:38:21.757 --> 00:38:23.350
That's not secure.

00:38:23.350 --> 00:38:24.958
That's not the way to do it.

00:38:24.958 --> 00:38:26.750
You also need a different
set of reporting,

00:38:26.750 --> 00:38:29.290
monitoring for
APIs that are being

00:38:29.290 --> 00:38:33.220
used by external
clients, shared APIs.

00:38:33.220 --> 00:38:35.200
Business people
are going to want

00:38:35.200 --> 00:38:38.105
to see what's the trend in
the number of developers

00:38:38.105 --> 00:38:40.480
that are using my APIs, the
number of different partners.

00:38:40.480 --> 00:38:44.500
Or for errors, is there
a particular developer

00:38:44.500 --> 00:38:47.830
that has built APIs that
is causing more errors?

00:38:47.830 --> 00:38:50.710
Different kinds of
monitoring and reporting

00:38:50.710 --> 00:38:53.140
is enabled by an API
Management platform.

00:38:53.140 --> 00:38:56.410
That's probably not something
you want on the internal APIs.

00:38:56.410 --> 00:39:00.520
So you do get some API
management capability

00:39:00.520 --> 00:39:03.610
in Service Mesh Infrastructure,
but it's probably not enough

00:39:03.610 --> 00:39:05.387
when you start to share out.

00:39:05.387 --> 00:39:07.220
GREG KUELGEN: So there's
some overlap there.

00:39:07.220 --> 00:39:08.678
So I'm going to go
back to latency.

00:39:08.678 --> 00:39:12.130
Again, my consumers
are super demanding.

00:39:12.130 --> 00:39:14.620
Don't I get latency incurred
with API Management?

00:39:14.620 --> 00:39:16.067
DINO CHIESA: Latency free zone.

00:39:16.067 --> 00:39:16.900
GREG KUELGEN: Again.

00:39:16.900 --> 00:39:17.608
DINO CHIESA: Yes.

00:39:17.608 --> 00:39:18.220
Magic.

00:39:18.220 --> 00:39:19.387
GREG KUELGEN: I'm impressed.

00:39:19.387 --> 00:39:21.910
DINO CHIESA: No, so there
is obviously a latency.

00:39:21.910 --> 00:39:24.320
Every time you add an additional
hop, there is a latency.

00:39:24.320 --> 00:39:26.680
And what we showed in
the demo, what you showed

00:39:26.680 --> 00:39:29.740
was a cloud-based API
Management Gateway.

00:39:29.740 --> 00:39:32.440
So it was running somewhere
on an external endpoint

00:39:32.440 --> 00:39:33.610
accessible to the internet.

00:39:33.610 --> 00:39:34.300
GREG KUELGEN: Magic.

00:39:34.300 --> 00:39:35.410
DINO CHIESA: Anything
that was calling

00:39:35.410 --> 00:39:38.410
in had to go through the public
network, get into that Gateway,

00:39:38.410 --> 00:39:42.760
and then into the internal mesh
or the services in the mesh.

00:39:42.760 --> 00:39:45.910
So yeah, if you have an internal
client and it's doing that,

00:39:45.910 --> 00:39:47.560
it's going out the
public network,

00:39:47.560 --> 00:39:48.870
coming in through that
gateway, and there's

00:39:48.870 --> 00:39:50.080
going to be a hop there.

00:39:50.080 --> 00:39:52.040
So yes, there can be.

00:39:52.040 --> 00:39:53.680
But not necessarily.

00:39:53.680 --> 00:39:56.230
So at this conference,
as you know,

00:39:56.230 --> 00:40:01.180
we've announced the
beta availability

00:40:01.180 --> 00:40:03.820
of the Apigee Hybrid
Gateway, which

00:40:03.820 --> 00:40:07.540
is the same Gateway that we
just saw you demonstrating.

00:40:07.540 --> 00:40:08.590
Same capabilities.

00:40:08.590 --> 00:40:11.960
But it is able to run inside
the Kubernetes cluster.

00:40:11.960 --> 00:40:13.368
So it's not a sidecar.

00:40:13.368 --> 00:40:15.160
It's not running right
next to the service.

00:40:15.160 --> 00:40:16.960
It's still a shared
Gateway, but it's

00:40:16.960 --> 00:40:19.660
in the cluster, which means
it's on the local network.

00:40:19.660 --> 00:40:20.660
It's going to be fast.

00:40:20.660 --> 00:40:22.327
It's going to be low
latency, and you're

00:40:22.327 --> 00:40:24.440
going to be able to stay
on the private network.

00:40:24.440 --> 00:40:26.740
So if you have
sensitive traffic,

00:40:26.740 --> 00:40:28.717
there's no regulatory
issue there as well.

00:40:28.717 --> 00:40:29.800
GREG KUELGEN: That's cool.

00:40:29.800 --> 00:40:30.860
DINO CHIESA: Yeah, really nice.

00:40:30.860 --> 00:40:32.290
So yes, there's some latency.

00:40:32.290 --> 00:40:34.210
Again, go with a cost
benefit analysis,

00:40:34.210 --> 00:40:36.010
and there's a
mitigation where you

00:40:36.010 --> 00:40:39.210
can run that Gateway inside
the Kubernetes cluster.

00:40:42.587 --> 00:40:44.670
GREG KUELGEN: So I think
we may have covered this,

00:40:44.670 --> 00:40:46.130
but do all of the
API requests hop

00:40:46.130 --> 00:40:47.420
out onto the public network?

00:40:47.420 --> 00:40:49.212
DINO CHIESA: I think
maybe we covered this.

00:40:49.212 --> 00:40:51.240
So the answer is,
not necessarily.

00:40:51.240 --> 00:40:53.750
If you use the Apigee
Sas service today,

00:40:53.750 --> 00:40:54.830
it's going to happen.

00:40:54.830 --> 00:40:59.750
But if you run that gateway
inside, then you don't.

00:40:59.750 --> 00:41:02.230
GREG KUELGEN: What
about, is Istio required?

00:41:02.230 --> 00:41:04.490
DINO CHIESA: So Istio is
something that you used.

00:41:04.490 --> 00:41:08.900
And I kind of broke it
down, did a quick overview

00:41:08.900 --> 00:41:09.950
of what it does.

00:41:09.950 --> 00:41:13.220
It's not actually required
in order to manage services.

00:41:13.220 --> 00:41:16.010
You can manage services,
different companies

00:41:16.010 --> 00:41:16.970
use different things.

00:41:16.970 --> 00:41:19.940
They may use their own approach.

00:41:19.940 --> 00:41:23.330
They may use HA proxy, they
may use Nginx, and couple

00:41:23.330 --> 00:41:25.370
that with some log
aggregation tools

00:41:25.370 --> 00:41:27.137
and call that their
Services Mesh.

00:41:27.137 --> 00:41:28.720
There's nothing wrong
with doing that.

00:41:28.720 --> 00:41:30.740
And, in fact, if a
company's large enough,

00:41:30.740 --> 00:41:33.320
there's going to be
heterogeneity in the approaches

00:41:33.320 --> 00:41:34.340
that they've taken.

00:41:34.340 --> 00:41:36.600
So it's not absolutely
required that you do Istio.

00:41:36.600 --> 00:41:38.540
We think Istio is a good idea.

00:41:38.540 --> 00:41:41.990
It's being built to address
the 80% case at least

00:41:41.990 --> 00:41:42.920
for most people.

00:41:42.920 --> 00:41:45.560
So we think it's
worth looking at,

00:41:45.560 --> 00:41:46.940
but it's not
absolutely required.

00:41:46.940 --> 00:41:50.330
You can still do Services
Mesh with your own toolset

00:41:50.330 --> 00:41:52.020
and compose that
with API Management

00:41:52.020 --> 00:41:54.470
according to the patterns
that we just described.

00:41:54.470 --> 00:41:56.210
That still is going
to work great.

00:41:58.750 --> 00:42:01.360
GREG KUELGEN: So I think this
is maybe my last question,

00:42:01.360 --> 00:42:03.670
but this sounds
super complicated.

00:42:03.670 --> 00:42:05.498
Do I really need
all of this stuff?

00:42:05.498 --> 00:42:07.040
DINO CHIESA: Nope,
you don't need it.

00:42:07.040 --> 00:42:08.410
If you don't want
to conduct business,

00:42:08.410 --> 00:42:09.210
you don't need any of this.

00:42:09.210 --> 00:42:10.640
GREG KUELGEN: All
right, awesome.

00:42:10.640 --> 00:42:11.140
There we go.

00:42:11.140 --> 00:42:14.180
And on that--

00:42:14.180 --> 00:42:15.020
DINO CHIESA: Look.

00:42:15.020 --> 00:42:16.708
As soon as you have
multiple services,

00:42:16.708 --> 00:42:19.000
you're going to want the
capabilities of a Service Mesh

00:42:19.000 --> 00:42:19.690
Infrastructure.

00:42:19.690 --> 00:42:20.773
You're going to want that.

00:42:20.773 --> 00:42:23.890
You want to protect the
transmissions between them,

00:42:23.890 --> 00:42:26.320
and that's the
best way to do it.

00:42:26.320 --> 00:42:28.030
As soon as you
start sharing APIs,

00:42:28.030 --> 00:42:30.340
you're going to want
API Management software.

00:42:30.340 --> 00:42:32.260
Now, if you're a startup, sure.

00:42:32.260 --> 00:42:35.080
Be thoughtful about the
things, the components,

00:42:35.080 --> 00:42:38.170
the tools that you embed
into your architecture.

00:42:38.170 --> 00:42:40.300
You don't want to build
the world at first.

00:42:40.300 --> 00:42:42.640
Maybe you're on
this MVP approach.

00:42:42.640 --> 00:42:43.820
OK, I get it.

00:42:43.820 --> 00:42:46.690
Maybe you can
economize, minimize,

00:42:46.690 --> 00:42:48.520
do it yourself on
different things.

00:42:48.520 --> 00:42:51.405
If you're an enterprise,
you're a big company,

00:42:51.405 --> 00:42:52.780
you have governance
requirements,

00:42:52.780 --> 00:42:54.610
you've got regulatory
requirements,

00:42:54.610 --> 00:42:58.150
you need tools, infrastructure
to help you govern and manage

00:42:58.150 --> 00:42:59.890
and control those
sorts of things,

00:42:59.890 --> 00:43:02.230
and you're probably going
to need both of these

00:43:02.230 --> 00:43:04.690
in the right doses now.

00:43:04.690 --> 00:43:06.970
So this is how we
think they compose,

00:43:06.970 --> 00:43:10.990
and this is probably a good
recommendation for most

00:43:10.990 --> 00:43:11.780
big companies.

00:43:11.780 --> 00:43:14.800
It's not about having cool
tools and cool infrastructure.

00:43:14.800 --> 00:43:16.660
GREG KUELGEN: And
I like cool tools.

00:43:16.660 --> 00:43:17.785
DINO CHIESA: I know you do.

00:43:17.785 --> 00:43:21.760
It's about getting
things done more quickly.

00:43:21.760 --> 00:43:24.400
Composing Service Mesh
Infrastructure and API

00:43:24.400 --> 00:43:27.232
Management allows the business
to innovate more quickly,

00:43:27.232 --> 00:43:28.940
and that's what we're
talking about here.

00:43:28.940 --> 00:43:30.760
GREG KUELGEN: Awesome.

00:43:30.760 --> 00:43:33.840
DINO CHIESA: So with that kind
of back and forth dialogue,

00:43:33.840 --> 00:43:38.290
Greg and I tried to summarize
the decision criteria

00:43:38.290 --> 00:43:41.890
for when you want to use
more or less API Management.

00:43:41.890 --> 00:43:45.230
I think the call on services
management is really simple.

00:43:45.230 --> 00:43:48.610
If you have more than just
a handful of services,

00:43:48.610 --> 00:43:51.070
you're going to want some
Service Mesh Infrastructure.

00:43:51.070 --> 00:43:52.820
You're going to want
something like Istio.

00:43:52.820 --> 00:43:55.493
At least Envoy, or
some other proxy,

00:43:55.493 --> 00:43:57.910
and you're probably going to
want a control plane as well.

00:43:57.910 --> 00:43:59.040
Istio is worth looking at.

00:43:59.040 --> 00:44:02.380
But for API Management, it's
a little bit more nuanced.

00:44:02.380 --> 00:44:05.800
Where you apply it
is maybe interesting.

00:44:05.800 --> 00:44:08.830
As we said, big enterprises are
probably going to be using it,

00:44:08.830 --> 00:44:10.150
but the question is where.

00:44:10.150 --> 00:44:10.960
At what layer?

00:44:10.960 --> 00:44:12.010
At what interface?

00:44:12.010 --> 00:44:15.490
And these are some of the
decision criteria summarizing

00:44:15.490 --> 00:44:17.560
what we just talked about.

00:44:17.560 --> 00:44:20.910
[MUSIC PLAYING]

